<p>Nobody comes here for software reviews, and I’m behind the crowd anyway, but I just wanted to say that I’m now using Ben McCarthy’s <a href="https://benricemccarthy.com/tripod/">Tripod</a> as my podcast player. It’s as good as all the real software reviewers have been saying.</p>
<p><img alt="iPhone running Tripod in Queue view" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261125-iPhone%20running%20Tripod%20in%20Queue%20view.png" title="iPhone running Tripod in Queue view" width="60%"/></p>
<p>It’s been about a year since bugs in Castro made me <a href="https://leancrew.com/all-this/2025/11/casting-about-again/">give up on it</a> and return to <a href="https://www.downcastapp.com/">Downcast</a>. Recently, a lesser Downcast bug—it doesn’t always delete an episode when I tell it to—has made me think about changing players again. Tripod came along at the right time with the right recommendations, and I happily subscribed after a short trial.</p>
<p>Like Castro, the key feature of Tripod is the Inbox, a place where the metadata for new episodes can go and where you can decide whether they’re worth listening to before downloading and putting them in your Queue. Unlike Castro, Tripod has adopted the convention of swiping left to delete episodes, whether downloaded or not. This makes it better than Castro, even if the bugs that drove me away from Castro have been fixed (which they may well have been over the past year).</p>
<p>Please note that I said new episodes <em>can</em> go into your Inbox, not that they <em>must</em> go into your Inbox. My default setting in Tripod is for new episodes to go to the Inbox for triage because I don’t listen to every episode of most of the shows I subscribe to. But there are some podcasts I listen to every episode of, and I have new episodes of these go directly to my Queue instead of to the Inbox. Here, for example, are my settings for <a href="https://relay.fm/designed/"><em>Designed in California</em></a>: its episodes go right to my Queue.</p>
<p><img alt="Tripod setting for a particular podcast" class="ss" src="https://leancrew.com/all-this/images2026/20261125-Tripod%20setting%20for%20a%20particular%20podcast.png" title="Tripod setting for a particular podcast" width="60%"/></p>
<p>Note that I have no limit on how many episodes can be in the Queue because new series of <em>Designed</em> come to me in batches. In contrast, although new episodes of <a href="https://www.apple.com/apple-news/"><em>Apple News Today</em></a> also go straight to my Queue, I give it a Queue Limit of 1 because old news is an oxymoron.</p>
<p>By the way, Tripod is pretty smart about my listening habits. Earlier today, I moved the latest episode of <a href="https://www.serioustrouble.show/podcast"><em>Serious Trouble</em></a> from the Inbox to my Queue, and Tripod asked if I wanted to change its settings, as I had moved every previous episode into the Queue. That’s how software should work.</p>
<p>Tripod has other nice features, like the kind of audio settings made <em>de rigueur</em> by <a href="https://overcast.fm/">Overcast</a>, chapters, clip sharing, and transcription. It’s <a href="https://benricemccarthy.com/tripod/">worth taking a look at</a>.</p>
